{"subtask_id":5,"findings":"Interaction patterns show a clear structural difference between the order and item perspectives. Order-level activities always bundle one order with multiple items/products: place order, confirm order, pay order, and payment reminder each involve 1 order and a median of 3 items. This means order-level performance aggregates several item trajectories, so delays in any item can affect the diagnosis for the whole order. Cross-object synchronization is especially high at pay order (median 166807.5s) and payment reminder (median 630589.0s) for orders-items, suggesting payment-related order delays are strongly tied to waiting for item/order convergence. Confirm order also shows substantial post-event lagging between orders and items (median 540478.0s), indicating that after order confirmation, item processing diverges and continues much later.

From the item perspective, item-processing activities are mostly one item with one product and one employee at pick item, item out of stock, and reorder item. These look more localized: median synchronization is 77563.5s for items-employees at pick item, 62674.5s for items-products at item out of stock, and 74605.5s for items-products at reorder item. The largest item-side cross-object dependencies appear when items are grouped into packages. Package activities involve many items per package: create package, send package, package delivered, and failed delivery have a median of 6 items per event, with median 5 to 5.5 products. Synchronization at package steps is moderate compared with order-payment steps, e.g. create package median 11394.5s, send package 26277.0s, package delivered 2419.0s, but lagging after package delivered is high at 260265.5s, suggesting delivery outcomes create downstream divergence across items/packages/employees.

